  /* data guides: scope metro (one page), city (per plan city) or county (per plan county where the numbers call for it) */
  const GUIDES = [
    { id: 'aclife', scope: 'metro', label: 'How long an AC lasts in Texas',
      h1: 'How Long Does an AC Last in Texas? What Dallas Fort Worth Data Shows', title: 'How Long Does an AC Last in Texas?',
      meta: 'Half of Texas central air conditioners are replaced by about {ac_med} years. What the survey data shows, why DFW summers wear systems and how to plan ahead.',
      answer: 'Half of Texas central air conditioners are replaced by about {ac_med} years, and about seven in ten by 20, based on a fit to the U.S. Energy Information Administration\'s 2020 survey of Texas homes. Across Dallas Fort Worth, an estimated {m_ge15} central systems are already 15 or older.',
      facts: ['ac_life', 'm_ge15', 'd100', 'hp_life'],
      body: ['<p>The Energy Information Administration asks households how old their central air conditioner is. Fitting a lifetime curve to the Texas answers gives a median age at replacement of about {ac_med} years. Heat pumps, which also heat, are replaced sooner, at about {hp_med} years; gas furnaces later, at about {furn_med}.</p><p>A system at 12 years has about {mrl12} years left on average; at 15, about {mrl15}. Those are averages across many homes: a well kept system in a shaded yard can run past 20, and one in a hot attic with a dirty coil can fail at 10.</p>',
        '<p>Texas air conditioners run many more hours a year than systems further north, and 2026 has had {d100} days at 100 degrees or more at DFW Airport through {through}, against about {d100n} in a normal year. Long run hours wear compressors and fan motors, attic heat stresses indoor coils and drains, and cottonwood and dust clog outdoor coils.</p>',
        '<p>Know the age of your system: the outdoor unit\'s data plate shows the model and serial number, and the serial usually encodes the year. Keep a maintenance record. Past 12 years, price a replacement before the next big repair, so the decision is made on your schedule and not in the first heat wave. Get written quotes that list the equipment, coil, line set, permit and the labor warranty terms.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'What shortens the life of an AC in Texas?', a: 'Long run hours, a hot attic, dirty coils and filters, low refrigerant from leaks and a system sized larger than the house needs.' },
        { q: 'Can an AC last 20 years?', a: 'Some do. About one in four Texas systems is still running at 20.' },
        { q: 'Is it better to replace before it fails?', a: 'Replacing on your schedule lets you compare quotes and choose the system, instead of taking the first available install during a heat wave.' },
        { q: 'How do I find my AC\'s age?', a: 'Read the serial number on the outdoor unit\'s data plate. Most brands encode the year of manufacture in it.' }] },
    { id: 'repairreplace', scope: 'metro', label: 'Repair or replace', table: 'mrl',
      h1: 'Repair or Replace Your AC? A Dallas Fort Worth Guide', title: 'Repair or Replace Your AC? A DFW Guide',
      meta: 'How to decide between repairing and replacing an air conditioner in Dallas Fort Worth: age, the life left, the size of the repair and what local permits show.',
      answer: 'Weigh the repair against the life the system has left. A Texas AC at 12 years has about {mrl12} years left on average, and at 15 about {mrl15}. A small repair on a young system is almost always worth it; a compressor or coil on a system past 12 often is not. Irving replacement permits from 2022 to 2025 had a median declared value of {t_med}.',
      facts: ['ac_life', 'mrl15', 'ticket', 'm_rep'],
      body: ['<p>The table shows how Texas air conditioners age, from a lifetime curve fitted to the Energy Information Administration survey. By 15 years, half have been replaced. The average life left falls every year, but more slowly than people expect, because the systems that survive to a given age tend to be the sturdier ones.</p>',
        '<p>One way to compare: divide the repair price by the average years left, and compare it with a new system\'s price divided by its expected life. If a repair buys each year at close to the cost of a year of a new system, replacing is the better value. Refrigerant matters too: an R22 system with a leak will need expensive refrigerant again, and a new condenser now needs a matched coil.</p>',
        '<p>Ask for both prices in writing on any large repair to a system past 10 years. Ask what failed, whether it is likely to recur and what the replacement includes. A contractor who quotes only a replacement, or only a repair, is giving you half the picture.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'Is it worth repairing a 15 year old AC?', a: 'For a small repair such as a capacitor, usually yes. For a compressor, a coil or a refrigerant leak, usually not: the average Texas system at 15 has about {mrl15} years left.' },
        { q: 'What does a new AC system cost in DFW?', a: 'Declared values on full system replacement permits in Irving from 2022 to 2025 had a median of {t_med}, with half between {t_p25} and {t_p75}.' },
        { q: 'Why did my repair quote include a replacement price?', a: 'Because the decision depends on both numbers. We show both so you can choose.' },
        { q: 'Does a new AC need a new furnace?', a: 'Not always. If the furnace is sound and its blower suits the new coil, it can stay.' }] },
    { id: 'heat', scope: 'metro', label: 'The 2026 summer and your AC',
      h1: 'The 2026 DFW Summer and Your AC: {d100} Days at 100 Degrees', title: 'The 2026 DFW Summer and Your AC',
      meta: '2026 has brought {d100} days at 100 degrees or more at DFW Airport through {through}. What a hot summer does to an AC and how to get yours through it.',
      answer: '2026 has had {d100} days at 100 degrees or more at DFW Airport through {through}, against about {d100n} in a normal year, {rank100}. Cooling demand is running {cddpct} above normal. In Fort Worth\'s permit records from 2012 to 2026, each extra 100 cooling degree days in a month came with about {fwheat} more HVAC permits.',
      facts: ['d100', 'cdd', 'fwheat', 'm_ge15'],
      body: ['<p>{d100} days at 100 degrees or hotter at DFW Airport so far in 2026 compares with about {d100n} in an average year. The record is {recd100} days, set in {recyr}. The hottest reading this year has been {tmax26} degrees.</p>',
        '<p>Heat does not break air conditioners on its own. It finds the weak part: a capacitor near the end of its life, a coil caked with dust, a system low on refrigerant. Long hot stretches mean long run times, and the calls pile up in the first heat wave and again late in summer, when worn parts finally give out.</p>',
        '<p>Change the filter monthly in peak summer. Keep the outdoor unit clear and rinse the coil gently with a hose, power off. Set the thermostat a few degrees higher when you are away rather than off. If the system runs constantly and cannot hold the setting after sunset, or ice forms on the lines, switch it off and call before the compressor is damaged.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'Why can my AC not keep up on 100 degree days?', a: 'Systems are sized for a design day, which in Dallas Fort Worth is close to 100 degrees. Above that, even a sound system can run constantly and drift a few degrees. If it cannot hold the setting after sunset, have it checked.' },
        { q: 'What temperature should I set in a heat wave?', a: 'A steady setting you can live with, raised a few degrees when you are away. Large setbacks make the system run for hours to recover.' },
        { q: 'Does a hot summer mean I need a new AC?', a: 'No. It means a weak part is more likely to fail. Maintenance catches most of them first.' },
        { q: 'Should I hose off my outdoor unit?', a: 'A gentle rinse from the inside out, with the power off, helps. Do not use a pressure washer on the fins.' }] },
    { id: 'r22guide', scope: 'metro', label: 'R22 in 2026',
      h1: 'R22 in 2026: What DFW Owners of Older AC Systems Should Know', title: 'R22 in 2026: What DFW Owners Should Know',
      meta: 'An estimated {m_r22} Dallas Fort Worth systems were installed before 2010 and likely run on R22. What the end of R22 production means for repairs and replacement.',
      answer: 'R22 production and import ended on January 1, 2020, so repairs on R22 systems depend on recovered and reclaimed refrigerant. An estimated {m_r22} systems across Dallas Fort Worth were installed before 2010, the R22 era. Running one is legal; recharging one keeps getting more expensive.',
      facts: ['m_r22', 'r22stop', 'a2l', 'ac_life'],
      body: ['<p>Systems installed before about 2010 were designed for R22. Across the 12 counties in and around Dallas Fort Worth, an estimated {m_r22} are still running, about {m_r22_sh} of all central systems. Every one of them is now 16 years old or more.</p>',
        '<p>The Clean Air Act phaseout ended R22 production and import in 2020. What remains is recovered from old systems and reclaimed, a supply that only shrinks. New systems changed twice: to R410A around 2010, and from January 1, 2025, to A2L refrigerants such as R454B and R32 for new split systems. An R22 condenser cannot simply be swapped for a new one without replacing the indoor coil and usually the line set.</p>',
        '<p>If your R22 system is sound, keep it maintained and budget for its replacement. If it leaks, get the recharge price by the pound and a replacement quote side by side. Never let anyone vent the old refrigerant; it must be recovered by a technician certified under EPA Section 608.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'How do I know if my AC uses R22?', a: 'Check the data plate on the outdoor unit. It lists the refrigerant, often as R22 or HCFC22.' },
        { q: 'Can I still buy R22?', a: 'Only recovered and reclaimed R22, charged by certified technicians. The price reflects a shrinking supply.' },
        { q: 'Do I have to replace my R22 system?', a: 'No. You can keep running and repairing it. Many owners replace it when a leak or a compressor failure makes the next repair poor value.' },
        { q: 'Will a new condenser work with my old coil?', a: 'No. New condensers need a matched indoor coil rated for their refrigerant.' }] },
    { id: 'buildyear', scope: 'city', label: 'Build year and your AC',
      h1: 'What Your {city} Home\'s Build Year Says About Its AC', title: '{city} Home Age and Your AC',
      meta: 'The median {city} home was built in {c_medyr}. What that means for the age of your AC and furnace, your refrigerant and when to plan a replacement.',
      answer: 'The median home in {city} was built in {c_medyr}. An estimated {c_ge15_sh} of the city\'s central systems are 15 or older, {c_r22_sh} date from before 2010 and likely run on R22, and {c_origwin_sh} are the builder\'s original system, now 10 to 25 years old.',
      facts: ['c_medyr', 'c_ge15', 'c_r22', 'c_origwin'],
      body: ['<p>{ybSentence}</p>',
        '<p>A home\'s build year sets the timeline for its systems. Homes built since 2016 mostly still have their first system. Homes built from 2000 to 2016 are in their first replacement wave now. Homes built before 2000 have usually replaced the original system at least once, and many still have original attic ducts. Systems installed before 2010 are likely on R22.</p>',
        '<p>Find your system\'s age on the outdoor unit\'s data plate and compare it with your home\'s build year. If the system is the original and past 12 years, plan the replacement. If the ducts are original to a house built before 2000, have them tested with the next system.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'How old is the average AC in {city}?', a: 'An estimated {c_ge15_sh} of central systems in {city} are 15 or older, based on Census housing data and Texas equipment age survey data.' },
        { q: 'Is my {city} home\'s AC on R22?', a: 'If it was installed before 2010, probably. The data plate on the outdoor unit says which refrigerant it uses.' },
        { q: 'Do newer {city} homes need replacements too?', a: 'Homes built from 2000 to 2016 are reaching their first replacement now, as builder systems pass 10 to 25 years.' },
        { q: 'When should I replace the ducts?', a: 'Test them with the next system if they are original to a home built before 2000, or if rooms never cool evenly.' }] },
    { id: 'hail', scope: 'county', label: 'Hail and your AC', need: k => (k.hail1_2016 || 0) >= 100,
      h1: 'Hail and Your AC in {county} County', title: 'Hail Damage to AC Units in {county} County',
      meta: '{county} County has had {k_hail} hail reports of one inch or larger since 2016. How hail damages outdoor AC units, what to check after a storm and how claims work.',
      answer: '{county} County has had {k_hail} hail reports of one inch or larger since 2016, {k_hail175} of them 1.75 inches or larger. Hail that size flattens the fins on outdoor AC units and can dent the coil. After a storm, photograph the unit, book an inspection and read your policy before you file.',
      facts: ['k_hail', 'k_hail175', 'k_big', 'k_sys'],
      body: ['<p>{k_hail} reports of hail one inch or larger have been logged in {county} County since 2016 in the NOAA Storm Events database, {k_hail175} of them golf ball size or larger. {k_bigSentence}</p>',
        '<p>The outdoor unit\'s coil is wrapped in thin aluminum fins. Hail flattens them, which blocks airflow across the coil, raises pressures and makes the compressor work harder. Heavy strikes can puncture tubing or crack the fan blades. Damage is easy to miss on a dusty coil, so it is best checked close up.</p>',
        '<p>After a storm, photograph the unit from all four sides with the date. Book an inspection; we measure and photograph the damage and give you a written repair or replacement quote. Your insurer decides coverage, so read your policy for the deductible and whether it pays replacement cost.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'Does insurance cover hail damage to an AC unit?', a: 'Many homeowners policies do, subject to the deductible. Your policy and your insurer decide.' },
        { q: 'Can I run my AC after hail?', a: 'Usually, if it starts and cools normally. If the fins are heavily flattened, the system works harder until it is repaired.' },
        { q: 'How long do I have to file a hail claim?', a: 'Policies set their own deadlines. Check yours and file promptly while the storm date is recent.' },
        { q: 'Can hail guards protect my AC?', a: 'Some guards reduce damage but can also restrict airflow if poorly designed. Ask before installing one.' }] },
    { id: 'furnace', scope: 'county', label: 'Furnace age and safety', need: k => (k.gas_sh || 0) >= 30 && (k.furn20 || 0) >= 1000,
      h1: 'Is Your Furnace Due? Gas Heat in {county} County', title: 'Furnace Age and Safety in {county} County',
      meta: '{k_gas} of {county} County homes heat with gas, and an estimated {k_furn20} furnaces are 20 or older. What that means for safety, repairs and replacement.',
      answer: '{k_gas} of homes in {county} County heat with natural gas, and an estimated {k_furn20} gas furnaces there are 20 years or older. Half of Texas gas furnaces are replaced by about {furn_med} years. An older furnace is not unsafe by age alone, but its heat exchanger and venting should be checked every fall.',
      facts: ['k_gas', 'k_furn20', 'furn_life', 'uri'],
      body: ['<p>North Texas winters are short, so furnaces run fewer hours and last longer than air conditioners. Even so, an estimated {k_furn20} furnaces in {county} County are 20 or older. Cold snaps still happen: during Winter Storm Uri, DFW Airport fell to 2 degrees below zero on February 16, 2021.</p>',
        '<p>What wears out is the heat exchanger, which keeps combustion gases out of the air you breathe, along with igniters, flame sensors, inducer motors and control boards. Cracks and poor venting are the safety issues; the rest are repairs.</p>',
        '<p>Have gas heat checked each fall, including a carbon monoxide test, and keep a working carbon monoxide alarm on every level of the house. If the furnace is past 20 and needs a major part, price a replacement too, and consider whether a heat pump should take over part of the heating when the AC is replaced.</p>'],
      faq: [
        { q: 'How long does a gas furnace last in Texas?', a: 'Half are replaced by about {furn_med} years. Many run longer because Texas winters are mild.' },
        { q: 'How do I know if my furnace is safe?', a: 'A safety check inspects the heat exchanger and venting and measures carbon monoxide. Keep working carbon monoxide alarms in the house.' },
        { q: 'Should I replace my furnace when I replace my AC?', a: 'If both are near the same age, doing both at once saves labor and matches the blower to the new coil.' },
        { q: 'What is a 95 percent furnace?', a: 'A condensing furnace that captures more of the heat from the gas. It needs plastic venting and a condensate drain, which not every attic installation can take.' }] },
  ];

